{"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2025, 10, 14]], "date-time": "2025-10-14T00:28:51Z", "timestamp": 1760401731619, "version": "build-2065373602"}, "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Walter de Gruyter GmbH", "issue": "2-3", "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "short-container-title": ["J. Study Hist. Jesus"], "published-print": {"date-parts": [[2025, 8, 21]]}, "abstract": "<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>\n<jats:p>The present article offers a rejoinder to the reviews of the book <jats:italic>They Suffered under Pontius Pilate: Jewish Anti-Roman Resistance and the Crosses at Golgotha</jats:italic>. The response replies individually to each of the essays by Christina Gousopoulos, Paul Middleton, Bruce Worthington, Warren Goldstein, and James Crossley. The important issues discussed include the relationship between History and Theology, ideological assumptions and positionality, the nature of the notion of ‘kingdom of God’, the historicity and character of the men crucified along with Jesus, the responsibility of Romans and Jewish authorities in Jesus’ (and others’) crucifixion, the causes of Jesus’ (and others’) crucifixion, the distortions and caricature of the hypothesis about a Jesus involved in anti-Roman resistance, and the potential impact and future of such a hypothesis.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1163/17455197-bja10055", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2025, 8, 24]], "date-time": "2025-08-24T03:10:26Z", "timestamp": 1756005026000}, "page": "196-235", "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["The Crux(es) of the Argument(s)"], "prefix": "10.1163", "volume": "23", "author": [{"given": "Fernando", "family": "Bermejo-Rubio", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "https://dx.doi.org/16757Department of Ancient History, uned, Madrid, Spain"}]}], "member": "374", "container-title": ["Journal for the Study of the Historical Jesus"], "link": [{"URL": "https://brill.com/view/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p196_007.xml", "content-type": "text/html", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "text-mining"}, {"URL": "https://brill.com/downloadpdf/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p196_007.xml", "content-type": "text/html", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "syndication"}, {"URL": "https://brill.com/downloadpdf/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p196_007.xml", "content-type": "unspecified", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "similarity-checking"}], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2025, 10, 13]], "date-time": "2025-10-13T09:36:49Z", "timestamp": 1760348209000}, "score": 0.0, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://brill.com/view/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p196_007.xml"}}, "subtitle": ["A Response to My Five Respondents"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[2025, 8, 21]]}, "references-count": 0, "journal-issue": {"issue": "2-3"}, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.1163/17455197-bja10055", "ISSN": ["1476-8690", "1745-5197"], "issn-type": [{"type": "print", "value": "1476-8690"}, {"type": "electronic", "value": "1745-5197"}], "published": {"date-parts": [[2025, 8, 21]]}, "_source": "crossref", "_target_issn": "1476-8690"}
{"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2025, 10, 14]], "date-time": "2025-10-14T00:28:07Z", "timestamp": 1760401687939, "version": "build-2065373602"}, "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Walter de Gruyter GmbH", "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "short-container-title": ["J. Study Hist. Jesus"], "published-print": {"date-parts": [[2025, 6, 18]]}, "abstract": "<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>\n<jats:p>In Jewish religious literature of the Second Temple period, coins as physical objects were closely associated with the sins that were committed through them. Such ‘tainted money’ could bring about bad luck or divine punishment to its owner according to the mindset of the authors. Some of the religious ideals reflected in early Christian texts can be interpreted as responses to the anxiety about sinful money too. Ascetic life in desert and voluntary poverty were both capable of neutralizing the sinful nature of coins. In particular, the image of John the Baptist in New Testament texts suggests that at the core of the Baptist’s success was anxiety about sinful money.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1163/17455197-bja10047", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2025, 6, 18]], "date-time": "2025-06-18T23:08:38Z", "timestamp": 1750288118000}, "page": "1-51", "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["Sinful Money: Attitudes to Coins in Second Temple Judaism and the Origins of Christianity"], "prefix": "10.1163", "author": [{"given": "Tamás", "family": "Visi", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "Kurt and Ursula Schubert Center for Jewish Studies, https://dx.doi.org/161955Palacký University, Olomouc, Czech Republic"}]}], "member": "374", "container-title": ["Journal for the Study of the Historical Jesus"], "link": [{"URL": "https://brill.com/view/journals/jshj/aop/article-10.1163-17455197-bja10047/article-10.1163-17455197-bja10047.xml", "content-type": "text/html", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "text-mining"}, {"URL": "https://brill.com/downloadpdf/journals/jshj/aop/article-10.1163-17455197-bja10047/article-10.1163-17455197-bja10047.xml", "content-type": "text/html", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "syndication"}, {"URL": "https://brill.com/downloadpdf/journals/jshj/aop/article-10.1163-17455197-bja10047/article-10.1163-17455197-bja10047.xml", "content-type": "unspecified", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "similarity-checking"}], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2025, 10, 13]], "date-time": "2025-10-13T09:37:16Z", "timestamp": 1760348236000}, "score": 0.0, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://brill.com/view/journals/jshj/aop/article-10.1163-17455197-bja10047/article-10.1163-17455197-bja10047.xml"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2025, 6, 18]]}, "references-count": 0, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.1163/17455197-bja10047", "ISSN": ["1476-8690", "1745-5197"], "issn-type": [{"type": "print", "value": "1476-8690"}, {"type": "electronic", "value": "1745-5197"}], "published": {"date-parts": [[2025, 6, 18]]}, "_source": "crossref", "_target_issn": "1476-8690"}
{"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2025, 10, 14]], "date-time": "2025-10-14T00:28:39Z", "timestamp": 1760401719455, "version": "build-2065373602"}, "reference-count": 1, "publisher": "Walter de Gruyter GmbH", "issue": "2-3", "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "short-container-title": ["J. Study Hist. Jesus"], "published-print": {"date-parts": [[2025, 5, 28]]}, "abstract": "<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>\n<jats:p>This is a review essay of Fernando Bermejo-Rubio’s book, <jats:italic>They Suffered under Pontius Pilate: Jewish Anti-Roman Resistance</jats:italic> (Lexington Books/Fortress Academic, 2023). The starting point for the book is the crucifixion of Jesus at Golgotha with him at the center and two bandits crucified on both sides of him. Since crucifixion was the Roman punishment for rebellion, Bermejo-Rubio conjectures that Jesus was the leader of a rebellion and that he must have had some connection with the two bandits. Bermejo-Rubio does this to counteract the predominant narrative in the Gospels that it was the priests, elders, and the scribes who were responsible for Jesus’ execution, in other words, that the Jews killed Jesus. This article argues that Bermejo-Rubio absolves the responsibility of the Jewish priest class and that it was most likely both them and the Romans who were responsible for Jesus’ death.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1163/17455197-bja10053", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2025, 5, 28]], "date-time": "2025-05-28T23:06:48Z", "timestamp": 1748473608000}, "page": "163-177", "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["Who Killed Jesus?"], "prefix": "10.1163", "volume": "23", "author": [{"given": "Warren S.", "family": "Goldstein", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "https://dx.doi.org/446212Center for Critical Research on Religion, Newton Centre, MA, USA"}]}], "member": "374", "reference": [{"year": "1987", "unstructured": "Josephus. The Works of Josephus: Complete and Unabridged, New Updated Edition. Edited by William Whiston. Peabody, MA: Hendrickson, 1987.", "series-title": "The Works of Josephus: Complete and Unabridged, New Updated Edition", "key": "CIT0001"}], "container-title": ["Journal for the Study of the Historical Jesus"], "link": [{"URL": "https://brill.com/view/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p163_005.xml", "content-type": "text/html", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "text-mining"}, {"URL": "https://brill.com/downloadpdf/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p163_005.xml", "content-type": "text/html", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "syndication"}, {"URL": "https://brill.com/downloadpdf/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p163_005.xml", "content-type": "unspecified", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "similarity-checking"}], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2025, 10, 13]], "date-time": "2025-10-13T09:36:40Z", "timestamp": 1760348200000}, "score": 0.0, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://brill.com/view/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p163_005.xml"}}, "subtitle": ["A Response to Fernando Bermejo-Rubio"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[2025, 5, 28]]}, "references-count": 1, "journal-issue": {"issue": "2-3"}, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.1163/17455197-bja10053", "ISSN": ["1476-8690", "1745-5197"], "issn-type": [{"type": "print", "value": "1476-8690"}, {"type": "electronic", "value": "1745-5197"}], "published": {"date-parts": [[2025, 5, 28]]}, "_source": "crossref", "_target_issn": "1476-8690"}
{"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2025, 10, 14]], "date-time": "2025-10-14T00:28:02Z", "timestamp": 1760401682316, "version": "build-2065373602"}, "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Walter de Gruyter GmbH", "issue": "2-3", "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "short-container-title": ["J. Study Hist. Jesus"], "published-print": {"date-parts": [[2025, 6, 30]]}, "abstract": "<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>\n<jats:p>This article reviews Fernando Bermejo-Rubio’s monograph, <jats:italic>They Suffered under Pontius Pilate: Jewish Anti-Roman Resistance and the Crosses at Golgotha</jats:italic> (2023). This book is the latest publication arguing for the ‘seditious Jesus’ hypothesis, the idea that Jesus was an armed revolutionary. It is argued that the volume rightly critiques some theological tendency in New Testament scholarship to downplay or ignore violence inherent in the Jesus tradition, but the argument that the men crucified with Jesus were either some of his disciples or sympathetic to his violent cause fails to convince. Despite arguing for historical minimalism in relation to the Gospel material, Bermejo-Rubio builds his case on the material he judges to be historical, but that is better explained by the imagination of the evangelists.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1163/17455197-bja10054", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2025, 6, 30]], "date-time": "2025-06-30T23:39:42Z", "timestamp": 1751326782000}, "page": "134-152", "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["How Much History is in the Passion Narratives?"], "prefix": "10.1163", "volume": "23", "author": [{"given": "Paul", "family": "Middleton", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "https://dx.doi.org/11965Theology and Religious Studies, University of Chester, Chester, UK"}]}], "member": "374", "container-title": ["Journal for the Study of the Historical Jesus"], "link": [{"URL": "https://brill.com/view/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p134_003.xml", "content-type": "text/html", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "text-mining"}, {"URL": "https://brill.com/downloadpdf/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p134_003.xml", "content-type": "text/html", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "syndication"}, {"URL": "https://brill.com/downloadpdf/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p134_003.xml", "content-type": "unspecified", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "similarity-checking"}], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2025, 10, 13]], "date-time": "2025-10-13T09:36:38Z", "timestamp": 1760348198000}, "score": 0.0, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://brill.com/view/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p134_003.xml"}}, "subtitle": ["Violence, Ideology, Historicity, and the Seditious Jesus Hypothesis"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[2025, 6, 30]]}, "references-count": 0, "journal-issue": {"issue": "2-3"}, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.1163/17455197-bja10054", "ISSN": ["1476-8690", "1745-5197"], "issn-type": [{"type": "print", "value": "1476-8690"}, {"type": "electronic", "value": "1745-5197"}], "published": {"date-parts": [[2025, 6, 30]]}, "_source": "crossref", "_target_issn": "1476-8690"}
{"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2025, 10, 14]], "date-time": "2025-10-14T00:28:05Z", "timestamp": 1760401685335, "version": "build-2065373602"}, "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Walter de Gruyter GmbH", "issue": "2-3", "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "short-container-title": ["J. Study Hist. Jesus"], "published-print": {"date-parts": [[2025, 5, 14]]}, "abstract": "<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>\n<jats:p>This is an invited review article for Fernando Bermejo-Rubio’s book entitled <jats:italic>They Suffered under Pontius Pilate: Jewish Anti-Roman Resistance and the Crosses at Golgotha</jats:italic>. Using the work of recent decolonial approaches to the study of religion, the author questions Bermejo-Rubio’s strict secular historiographical approach to historical Jesus research, looking instead for ways in which secular and theological approaches can be co-constitutive.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1163/17455197-bja10052", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2025, 5, 14]], "date-time": "2025-05-14T23:05:10Z", "timestamp": 1747263910000}, "page": "153-162", "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["There is No ‘They’ without a ‘He’"], "prefix": "10.1163", "volume": "23", "author": [{"given": "Bruce", "family": "Worthington", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "Assistant Professor, https://dx.doi.org/26451Department of Cultural and Religious Studies, The Chinese University of Hong Kong, Hong Kong, SAR China"}]}], "member": "374", "container-title": ["Journal for the Study of the Historical Jesus"], "link": [{"URL": "https://brill.com/view/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p153_004.xml", "content-type": "text/html", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "text-mining"}, {"URL": "https://brill.com/downloadpdf/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p153_004.xml", "content-type": "text/html", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "syndication"}, {"URL": "https://brill.com/downloadpdf/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p153_004.xml", "content-type": "unspecified", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "similarity-checking"}], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2025, 10, 13]], "date-time": "2025-10-13T09:36:46Z", "timestamp": 1760348206000}, "score": 0.0, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://brill.com/view/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p153_004.xml"}}, "subtitle": ["A Response to Fernando Bermejo-Rubio"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[2025, 5, 14]]}, "references-count": 0, "journal-issue": {"issue": "2-3"}, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.1163/17455197-bja10052", "ISSN": ["1476-8690", "1745-5197"], "issn-type": [{"type": "print", "value": "1476-8690"}, {"type": "electronic", "value": "1745-5197"}], "published": {"date-parts": [[2025, 5, 14]]}, "_source": "crossref", "_target_issn": "1476-8690"}
{"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2025, 10, 14]], "date-time": "2025-10-14T00:28:06Z", "timestamp": 1760401686669, "version": "build-2065373602"}, "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Walter de Gruyter GmbH", "issue": "2-3", "license": [{"start": {"date-parts": [[2025, 5, 28]], "date-time": "2025-05-28T00:00:00Z", "timestamp": 1748390400000}, "content-version": "unspecified", "delay-in-days": 0, "URL": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"}], "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "short-container-title": ["J. Study Hist. Jesus"], "published-print": {"date-parts": [[2025, 5, 28]]}, "abstract": "<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>\n<jats:p>This article looks at the history of the idea of an insurgent, seditious Jesus in light of Fernando Bermejo-Rubio’s <jats:italic>They Suffered under Pontius Pilate: Jewish Anti-Roman Resistance and the Crosses at Golgotha</jats:italic> (Lanham: Lexington Books, 2023). The focus is on reading the history of scholarship in its cultural and historical contexts, looking at why the insurgent Jesus and its critics came to the fore when they did. The article ends with final reflections on the language and ideas relating to insurgency and resistance in recent years and what impact they may have on historical Jesus studies.</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1163/17455197-bja10050", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2025, 5, 28]], "date-time": "2025-05-28T23:06:48Z", "timestamp": 1748473608000}, "page": "178-195", "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["The Past, Present, and Future of an Insurgent Jesus"], "prefix": "10.1163", "volume": "23", "author": [{"given": "James", "family": "Crossley", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "https://dx.doi.org/2152Faculty of Divinity, University of Cambridge, Cambridge, UK"}, {"name": "mf Oslo, Oslo, Norway"}]}], "member": "374", "container-title": ["Journal for the Study of the Historical Jesus"], "link": [{"URL": "https://brill.com/view/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p178_006.xml", "content-type": "text/html", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "text-mining"}, {"URL": "https://brill.com/downloadpdf/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p178_006.xml", "content-type": "text/html", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "syndication"}, {"URL": "https://brill.com/downloadpdf/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p178_006.xml", "content-type": "unspecified", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "similarity-checking"}], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2025, 10, 13]], "date-time": "2025-10-13T09:36:38Z", "timestamp": 1760348198000}, "score": 0.0, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://brill.com/view/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p178_006.xml"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[2025, 5, 28]]}, "references-count": 0, "journal-issue": {"issue": "2-3"}, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.1163/17455197-bja10050", "ISSN": ["1476-8690", "1745-5197"], "issn-type": [{"type": "print", "value": "1476-8690"}, {"type": "electronic", "value": "1745-5197"}], "published": {"date-parts": [[2025, 5, 28]]}, "_source": "crossref", "_target_issn": "1476-8690"}
{"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2025, 10, 14]], "date-time": "2025-10-14T00:28:02Z", "timestamp": 1760401682425, "version": "build-2065373602"}, "reference-count": 0, "publisher": "Walter de Gruyter GmbH", "issue": "2-3", "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "short-container-title": ["J. Study Hist. Jesus"], "published-print": {"date-parts": [[2025, 5, 7]]}, "abstract": "<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>\n<jats:p>This article reflects on the key contributions of Fernando Bermejo-Rubio’s recent monograph, which offers a fresh take on the ‘Seditious Jesus Hypothesis’ (<jats:sc>sjh</jats:sc>) by imagining Jesus and his collaborators as participants in an anti-Roman resistance movement. This response explores the implications of the <jats:sc>sjh</jats:sc>, the significance of the collective crucifixion, and inquires: what is good historiography, especially in studies of the historical Jesus?</jats:p>", "DOI": "10.1163/17455197-bja10051", "type": "journal-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2025, 5, 7]], "date-time": "2025-05-07T23:28:13Z", "timestamp": 1746660493000}, "page": "115-133", "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 0, "title": ["And Then There Were (At Least) Three …"], "prefix": "10.1163", "volume": "23", "author": [{"given": "Christina", "family": "Gousopoulos", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [{"name": "https://dx.doi.org/7938Department for the Study of Religion, University of Toronto, Toronto, Canada"}]}], "member": "374", "container-title": ["Journal for the Study of the Historical Jesus"], "link": [{"URL": "https://brill.com/view/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p115_002.xml", "content-type": "text/html", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "text-mining"}, {"URL": "https://brill.com/downloadpdf/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p115_002.xml", "content-type": "text/html", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "syndication"}, {"URL": "https://brill.com/downloadpdf/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p115_002.xml", "content-type": "unspecified", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "similarity-checking"}], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2025, 10, 13]], "date-time": "2025-10-13T09:36:36Z", "timestamp": 1760348196000}, "score": 0.0, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "https://brill.com/view/journals/jshj/23/2-3/article-p115_002.xml"}}, "subtitle": ["A Collective Crucifixion, the ‘Seditious Jesus Hypothesis’, and Writing Good Historiography"], "issued": {"date-parts": [[2025, 5, 7]]}, "references-count": 0, "journal-issue": {"issue": "2-3"}, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.1163/17455197-bja10051", "ISSN": ["1476-8690", "1745-5197"], "issn-type": [{"type": "print", "value": "1476-8690"}, {"type": "electronic", "value": "1745-5197"}], "published": {"date-parts": [[2025, 5, 7]]}, "_source": "crossref", "_target_issn": "1476-8690"}
